1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,320
This guy's bank paid way too damn well. Guess why.

2
00:00:03,980 --> 00:00:07,460
Here's the trick first. New customers' money paid off the old customers.

3
00:00:07,940 --> 00:00:11,540
Like paying back one friend with cash you borrowed from the next friend. Forever.

4
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:17,260
Now meet the guy. Allen Stanford, a Texan with a bank on the island of Antigua.

5
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:23,200
Now his bank sold CDs, certificates of deposit. You lock your money up for a fixed return.

6
00:00:23,640 --> 00:00:27,000
But his bank promised more than normal banks paid. Shocker.

7
00:00:27,580 --> 00:00:30,860
Hit like if you've ever seen a deal and thought, nah, that's cap.

8
00:00:31,640 --> 00:00:36,260
People still bought more than seven billion dollars of those CDs, over about twenty years.

9
00:00:36,820 --> 00:00:40,560
That's almost a million bucks a day, every day, for twenty years.

10
00:00:41,100 --> 00:00:44,920
But prosecutors said the returns were inflated, puffed up, not real.

11
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:48,380
Send this to the friend who keeps chasing the highest rate on the internet.

12
00:00:48,960 --> 00:00:53,700
Then in two thousand nine, the SEC, the government's investing watchdog, sued him.

13
00:00:54,080 --> 00:00:57,420
They called it a massive ongoing fraud. Those are their words.

14
00:00:57,700 --> 00:01:02,860
In two thousand twelve, a Houston jury found him guilty on thirteen of fourteen counts.

15
00:01:03,800 --> 00:01:05,840
Prosecutors asked for two hundred thirty years.

16
00:01:06,340 --> 00:01:09,860
His lawyers asked for about forty-one months. Bro, months.

17
00:01:10,560 --> 00:01:13,440
Follow if you want every scam broken down, math first.

18
00:01:13,960 --> 00:01:17,100
The judge sentenced him to a hundred and ten years in prison.

19
00:01:17,440 --> 00:01:22,140
Plus a five point nine billion dollar money judgment, a court order saying he owes it.

20
00:01:22,520 --> 00:01:27,060
So yeah, that bank paid way too well, and the next customer paid for it.

21
00:01:27,960 --> 00:01:30,780
A hundred and ten years. Enough? One word.

